                            <comment id="501987" author="JIRAUSER20209" created="Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:33:37 +0000">&lt;h4&gt;&lt;a name="Analysis"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Analysis&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some concurrent call sites for peer discovery could still enqueue a peer into the `ln_dc_request` queue after `lnet_peer_discovery_stop()` has already drained and asserted the discovery queue is empty. This can cause the extra reference taken by `lnet_peer_queue_for_discovery()` to never be released, leading to an `lnet_peer` leak, and an attached `lnet_ping_buffer` leak as well when push data had already been received, ultimately being reported as "Portals memory leaked" when the module is unloaded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The affected interfaces include:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul class="alternate" type="square"&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;`lnet_peer_push_event`&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;`lnet_push_update_to_peers`&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;`lnet_peer_queue_message`&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;`LNetPeerDiscovered`&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Since the race window is very short, the probability of hitting this issue in normal testing is extremely low. By manually adding two gate points for `lnet_peer_push_event()` and `lnet_peer_discovery_stop()` to control the timing sequence, the memory leak can be reliably reproduced.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;a name="FixStrategy"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Fix Strategy&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add a synchronization mechanism: &lt;br/&gt;
Re-check `ln_dc_state == LNET_DC_STATE_RUNNING` within the same `lnet_net_lock/EX` critical section where the actual insertion operation is performed.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
